<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE node PUBLIC "-//freedesktop//DTD D-BUS Object Introspection 1.0//EN"
 "http://www.freedesktop.org/standards/dbus/1.0/introspect.dtd">
<!--
  Typed-result sub-interface for a ReadCertificates Operation1. Exported on the
  same object path as the parent Operation1. The Result signal fires once,
  before Operation1.Finished, and only when the operation completed
  successfully (status == Ok).

  NO DER crosses the wire (the agent does all X.509 parsing; clients render
  strings and link neither LM nor OpenSSL). Each entry:

    s   certId          opaque stable unique id = SHA-256(DER); the handle Sign() takes
    b   signingCapable  paired on-card private key AND a signing-suitable keyUsage
                        (digitalSignature/nonRepudiation, or keyUsage absent)
    a{sa{s(ssv)}} fields   group -> field -> (labelKey, labelFallback, value)
        Deliberately the (ssv) shape — NOT Identity1's (sssv): no redundant
        type-string (the frozen field-key namespace carries the semantics).
        group keys:  subject | issuer | validity | publicKey | cert | ext |
                     basicConstraints | san | ian | crlDp | aia |
                     certificatePolicies | eku | security
        field keys:  subject/issuer: cn,o,ou,dn ; validity: notBefore,notAfter
                     publicKey: algorithm,sizeBits,curveOid
                     cert: serial,version,signatureAlgorithm (falls back to the
                       OID when the description is empty),
                       subjectKeyIdentifier,authorityKeyIdentifier (colon-hex,
                       the clean key-id bytes, NOT the raw extnValue dump)
                     ext: <oid-dotted> (value = uppercase hex of the raw
                       extension; a critical extension's labelFallback carries
                       a " (Critical)" suffix; the 10 OIDs served by a typed
                       group below are never duplicated here)
                     basicConstraints: isCa ("true"/"false"), pathLen (present
                       only when the certificate carries a path length constraint)
                     san / ian: ordinal-keyed fields (<type-token><n>, e.g.
                       "dns0", "email1") — one per SubjectAltName/IssuerAltName
                       GeneralName entry; value is the decoded string form, or
                       uppercase hex of the raw DER for a composite variant
                       (OtherName/X400Address/DirectoryName/EdiPartyName) the
                       parser does not decode further
                     crlDp: url<n> — one field per CRL distribution point URI
                     aia: ocsp<n> / caIssuers<n> — one field per AIA
                       OCSP-responder / CA-Issuers URI
                     certificatePolicies: policy<n> — one field per policy OID
                       (friendly name, else dotted-decimal; no qualifiers)
                     eku: usage<n> — one field per ExtendedKeyUsage purpose,
                       rendered by NAME through the OID database (dotted
                       decimal when it has no entry). Does NOT replace the
                       extendedKeyUsageOids member below, which stays the
                       dotted list a caller matches on
                     critical: every typed extension group above
                       (basicConstraints, san, ian, crlDp, aia,
                       certificatePolicies, eku) may also carry this field —
                       labelKey EMPTY, labelFallback "Critical", value "true"
                       — present ONLY when that extension is critical. A group
                       carries no label on this wire, so the marker rides a
                       field; the empty labelKey is what marks it as group
                       metadata rather than a row to render. Absent means not
                       critical; it is never emitted with a false value
                     security: one field per security-status token (see
                       trustStatus below), keyed by the token itself, value ==
                       the token — the lawful dict-key-growth route for
                       securityStatus (there is no separate wire member for it)
        values are UTF-8 strings (dates ISO-8601 UTC, serial/ext colon/plain hex).
        One further group, "diagnostic", is emitted INSTEAD of all of the
        above (and alone) when the agent cannot parse a certificate's DER at
        all: it carries a single "parseError" field with the parser's own
        message, and that cert's signingCapable stays false with an empty
        chainSubjectCns. It is listed apart because it is a failure channel,
        not part of the certificate vocabulary — but a client decodes it
        through exactly the same generic path.
    u   keyUsageBits    stable bitmask (bit i per X.509 KeyUsage ordinal); client localizes
    as  extendedKeyUsageOids
    as  chainSubjectCns  ordered leaf..root subject CNs (display only). NOTE: until
                         the chain ladder lands this is just [leaf CN].
    u   trustStatus     0 Trusted, 1 UntrustedRoot, 2 BrokenChain,
                        3 InvalidCertificate, 4 Expired, 5 Revoked,
                        6 OfflineUnverified, 255 Unknown. Computed by the
                        agent's TrustVerifier seam (LM Trust::TrustStore,
                        configured from TslSources/TslCacheDir); no TSL source
                        configured (or the trust store unavailable) yields 6
                        OfflineUnverified. Revoked (5) is reserved vocabulary —
                        no revocation (OCSP/CRL) check is wired yet, so the
                        agent never emits it today. Raw u32 pass-through;
                        clients must map any value they do not recognise to
                        Unknown, never fail the decode.
                        Matching security-status tokens (closed vocabulary:
                        "trusted", "untrusted-root", "broken-chain", "invalid",
                        "expired", "revoked", "offline-unverified") ride the
                        "security" fields group above.

  Late-subscriber recovery — GetResult():
    The Result signal is a one-shot: a client that learns the op path only
    from the ReadCertificates() return may subscribe too late (a hot card
    session finishes the read faster than the client's match rules install)
    and would lose the payload forever. GetResult() re-serves the retained
    certificate array — the exact Result payload — while the operation is
    still within the cleanup-grace window AND finished Ok. Returns
    org.librescrs.Agent.Error.NoResult once the grace window has elapsed or
    when the operation did not finish Ok. Same recovery contract as
    Sign1.GetResult. The retained certificate metadata lives only in agent
    RAM until the operation is evicted (cleanup-grace GC, card removal,
    owner disconnect) — never persisted; same retention policy as the Sign1
    artifact.
-->
<node>
  <interface name="org.librescrs.Agent.Operation.Certificates1">
    <signal name="Result">
      <arg name="certificates" type="a(sba{sa{s(ssv)}}uasasu)"/>
    </signal>

    <method name="GetResult">
      <arg name="certificates" type="a(sba{sa{s(ssv)}}uasasu)" direction="out"/>
    </method>
  </interface>
</node>
